Context (pp. 38-40 of this batch, Longue Marche sequel, §49-50): Grothendieck is writing the 'équation des lacets' (loop equation) for a scheme Σ_{ρ,σ}. It is built from a rank-2 lattice, or a twisted constant group scheme, M with basis λ0, λ1. The lattice carries automorphisms ρ (order 3), σ (order 2) and τ = −1. This is the familiar picture of the elliptic stabilisers in the modular group: ρ is the order-3 rotation and σ the involution, acting on a rank-2 lattice. On the previous page, conditions a) and b) of (17) were rewritten additively. They say ξ is locally a 'twisted coboundary' α0 − ρ(α0), shifted by −λ1 when ν3 = 1, and η0 = β0 − σ(β0) (eq. 28).

On this page (29) restates this as image conditions:
- ξ ∈ Im(1−ρ) when ν3 = 0, and ξ ∈ −λ1 + Im(1−ρ) when ν3 = 1;
- η0 ∈ Im(1−σ).

In the basis λ0, λ1, with columns giving the images of the basis vectors:
- ρ = [[0,−1],[1,−1]], so ρλ0 = λ1 and ρλ1 = −λ0−λ1; the characteristic polynomial is x²+x+1;
- σ = [[0,1],[1,0]], swapping λ0 and λ1;
- τ = −I;
- 1−ρ = [[1,1],[−1,2]] and 1−σ = [[1,−1],[−1,1]].

Hence det(1−ρ) = 3 and det(1−σ) = 0. Neither map is an automorphism. 1−ρ is invertible away from 3 and degenerates in characteristic 3, where M/(1−ρ)M ≅ Z/3. 1−σ has rank 1 in every characteristic, with image Z(λ0−λ1). Cohomologically: N_ρ = 1+ρ+ρ² = 0 on M, so H¹(Z/3, M) = M/(1−ρ)M ≅ Z/3. Also ker(1+σ) = Im(1−σ), so H¹(Z/2, M) = 0, since M is a permutation module.

Conclusion drawn: it is better to take as unknowns ('primitives') not ξ, η0 but the discrete 'primitives' α0, β0 themselves, in place of the earlier γ2, γ3, γ'. This leads on the next page to a finer scheme Σ̃_{ρ,σ} parametrised by (ν, ν', ν2, ν3, α0, β0). Its loop equation is (1−ρ)α0 = (1−σ)β0 + (ν−ν3)λ1. The next page remarks that Σ_{ρ,σ} is neither smooth nor (apparently) connected in characteristics 2 and 3, whereas Σ̃ seems smooth. It proposes reorganising §49 accordingly.
